WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.494
Two services in a mesh.

2
00:00:04.494 --> 00:00:08.004
A checkout app calls a fraud check over HTTP.

3
00:00:08.233 --> 00:00:10.986
Nobody changes the app's code or its URLs.

4
00:00:10.986 --> 00:00:16.097
The app still sends its request to fraud-check, as if nothing were in between.

5
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:20.843
In sidecar mode, every Pod gets an extra container: an Envoy proxy.

6
00:00:20.843 --> 00:00:26.404
The containers of a Pod share one network, so the proxy can see the app's traffic.

7
00:00:26.633 --> 00:00:29.934
A proxy only helps if traffic passes through it.

8
00:00:29.934 --> 00:00:37.292
So before the app starts, an init container or a CNI plugin writes iptables rules inside the Pod's network.

9
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:42.024
They are the same kernel packet rules kube-proxy writes.

10
00:00:42.024 --> 00:00:49.780
Istio's rules send outgoing connections to Envoy on port 15001, and incoming ones to port 15006.

11
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:51.408
Watch one call.

12
00:00:51.408 --> 00:00:54.232
The app opens a connection to fraud-check.

13
00:00:54.433 --> 00:01:01.512
The kernel matches the outgoing rule, and quietly hands the connection to Envoy, on port 15001.

14
00:01:01.512 --> 00:01:03.151
The app never notices.

15
00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:06.301
So how does Envoy know where the app meant to go?

16
00:01:06.301 --> 00:01:12.169
The kernel remembers the original address of every redirected connection, and Envoy reads it back.

17
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.666
Now follow the request inside Envoy.

18
00:01:15.666 --> 00:01:20.872
Downstream is the side that sends requests to Envoy: here, the checkout app.

19
00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:25.523
Upstream is the side Envoy sends them on to: the fraud check Pods.

20
00:01:25.733 --> 00:01:30.080
A listener accepts the connection, and runs its filter chain.

21
00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:33.072
Here, that is the HTTP connection manager.

22
00:01:33.072 --> 00:01:35.780
It turns the bytes into HTTP requests.

23
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.742
Then it runs the HTTP filters in order.

24
00:01:39.742 --> 00:01:44.957
The router comes last, because it is the filter that sends the request upstream.

25
00:01:45.167 --> 00:01:47.464
The router looks up the route table.

26
00:01:47.464 --> 00:01:49.953
A virtual host matches the Host header.

27
00:01:49.953 --> 00:01:53.080
Inside it, the first route whose match fits wins.

28
00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:54.911
The route names a cluster.

29
00:01:54.911 --> 00:01:58.940
In Envoy, a cluster is a group of upstream hosts for one service.

30
00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:01.853
It has nothing to do with a Kubernetes cluster.

31
00:02:02.067 --> 00:02:06.869
The cluster's load balancer picks one endpoint, a single address and port.

32
00:02:06.869 --> 00:02:12.320
It sends the request over a pooled connection, one that Envoy keeps open and reuses.

33
00:02:14.733 --> 00:02:17.421
Envoy's own call now leaves the Pod.

34
00:02:17.421 --> 00:02:24.962
It passes the same iptables rules, but they skip traffic from the proxy's own user, ID 1337 in Istio.

35
00:02:24.962 --> 00:02:29.068
Otherwise the call would loop straight back into Envoy.

36
00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:32.743
Between the Pods, the two proxies use mutual TLS.

37
00:02:32.743 --> 00:02:37.100
Both sides show a certificate, so each knows who the other is.

38
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:44.847
In the fraud check Pod, the incoming rule sends the connection to its Envoy, on port 15006.

39
00:02:45.067 --> 00:02:51.751
That second proxy does the same work for incoming calls, then passes the request to the fraud check app.

40
00:02:51.967 --> 00:02:54.031
The app made a plain HTTP call.

41
00:02:54.031 --> 00:02:58.892
The proxies add the timeout, the retries, the encryption and the metrics.

42
00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:01.939
Where does all this config come from?

43
00:03:01.939 --> 00:03:04.973
Pods start and stop all day, each with a new IP.

44
00:03:04.973 --> 00:03:09.967
A mesh may have hundreds of Envoys that must learn every change within seconds.

45
00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:14.146
Each Envoy starts from a small static file, the bootstrap.

46
00:03:14.146 --> 00:03:17.820
It says where its config server is: the control plane.

47
00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:19.521
In Istio, that is istiod.

48
00:03:19.733 --> 00:03:27.091
The control plane watches the Kubernetes API for Services, EndpointSlices and your routing objects.

49
00:03:27.091 --> 00:03:32.739
It turns them into Envoy objects, and pushes them to every proxy, over gRPC.

50
00:03:32.967 --> 00:03:38.678
The APIs that carry them are called xDS, one for each kind of object.

51
00:03:38.678 --> 00:03:40.333
LDS sends listeners.

52
00:03:40.333 --> 00:03:42.237
RDS sends route tables.

53
00:03:42.237 --> 00:03:43.809
CDS sends clusters.

54
00:03:44.033 --> 00:03:48.384
EDS sends endpoints, the Pod IPs behind each cluster.

55
00:03:48.384 --> 00:03:50.271
They change most often.

56
00:03:50.271 --> 00:03:54.293
And SDS sends secrets: TLS certificates and keys.

57
00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:55.441
Order matters.

58
00:03:55.441 --> 00:04:00.956
A route that names a cluster Envoy has not received yet sends its traffic nowhere.

59
00:04:01.167 --> 00:04:08.363
So many control planes send everything over one stream, called ADS, with clusters before the routes that use them.

60
00:04:08.567 --> 00:04:10.174
Each object is separate.

61
00:04:10.174 --> 00:04:18.543
When a new fraud check Pod starts, the control plane adds its endpoint over EDS, without touching the listener or the routes.

62
00:04:18.767 --> 00:04:24.541
And if the control plane goes down, the proxies keep serving with the last config they received.

63
00:04:24.541 --> 00:04:26.887
You only lose the ability to change it.

64
00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:32.460
iptables catches the call, and Envoy reads the original address.

65
00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:37.921
Then listener, route, cluster, endpoint, and mutual TLS to the other proxy.

66
00:04:37.921 --> 00:04:40.833
The control plane feeds it all over xDS.
